+
+### The validation programme's verdict — supersedes screenshots and the H-exponent gate
+
+A same-rectangle, stage-by-stage comparison against real DTMs now exists and is the primary
+acceptance instrument for this plan: `docs/terrain-validation-2026-07.md` (S0–S4 measurement, three
+terrain classes, reproduction commands in its §9), `terrain-service/docs/geomorph-validation.md`
+(which metrics can detect a fake and which cannot), `terrain_service/geomorph/` (the metric
+package), `terrain-service/tools/earth_reference.py` +
+`terrain-service/data/earth_reference/manifest.json` (12 real sites, 8
+classes, 9 with USGS 3DEP 1 m bare-earth DTM), and the stage-dump tools (`vxc_stagedump` +
+`terrain-service/tools/dump_stage_heightfields.py`, five stages: S0 raw 30 m diffusion tile, S1 bake
+output at 1.875 m, S2 client carrier only, S3 client full surface, S4 voxelised).
+
+**The headline result, in one sentence: the bake is a good plain, and the client destroys it, but
+only on gentle ground.** Against a real Illinois till plain, the bake (S1) tracks mean slope to
+within 12% at *every* rung from 1.875 m to 30 m (2.041/1.959/1.800/1.518/1.148° against
+2.134/1.943/1.728/1.523/1.301°). The client's detail pass (S3) then multiplies the 1.875 m rung by
+3.1× (2.04° → 6.27°) while leaving every coarser rung within 1% of the bake — "the defect is one
+octave wide, and it is introduced by the client's detail pass." The same pass on an alpine tile adds
+only +3.3% mean slope, because detail amplitude is conditioned on the carrier's *gradient*
+(`slopeScaleQ10`), not on the landform's own relief: ~0.3–0.6 m RMS of added roughness is 4.4% of a
+plain's own 6.7 m detrended σ and 0.36% of a mountain's 166 m σ, so the identical absolute roughness
+is landform-defining on a plain and rounding error on a mountain. Consequence: the finished plain
+measures `frac_ridge_peak = 0.352` at a 1.875 m posting (Illinois 0.073, Llano 0.054) with **zero
+flat cells in 1.19 M samples** (Llano is 55% flat at the same threshold) — **our own alpine tile
+measures ridge+peak 0.061, so the finished plain is six times more ridged than the finished
+mountain**, and a classifier handed both surfaces would call the plain the mountain.
+
+**The bake has a second, independent defect the client's detail pass masks rather than fixes.** At
+30 m the bake actually *improves* concavity — alpine theta moves from the input's 0.194 to 0.207,
+landing on the Alps' own 0.203, meaning incision does the right thing at the scale it inherited.
+Below 30 m, in the band the bake invents, theta collapses to **0.028–0.046** against **0.177–0.318**
+for matched real 1 m DTMs at the identical cell size (Teton 0.177, Iowa 0.318) — a 4–7× deficit that
+exists only in the band the bake itself creates. The honest target by this method is **0.18–0.32**,
+not the bake's own `m/n = 0.5625` prediction (that prediction is a steady-state attractor a one-pass
+solve is not obliged to reach — see "Two structural conclusions" under Layer 1 above — and the
+measured value sits on the *wrong side* of the input, not merely short of the attractor). The client
+then *raises* theta by adding detail (0.028 → 0.078 on alpine), which is a side effect of the
+slope–area regression rather than a fix: the same pass takes `curvature_asymmetry` down toward the
+Gaussian-fake band.
+
+**The ridge deficit is 6–10× and is born in B0+B1, before any incision runs.** At a matched 10-cell
+(18.75 m) lookout, the bake's fine tier under-produces ridge+peak by **10.7× on alpine** (0.0031 vs.
+a real 0.0332) and **2.6–2.9× on plains** (0.0166 vs. 0.0434–0.0484); the client's carrier alone
+(S2) reproduces the bake's fractions to within 0.001 in every class, so **the client inherits this
+defect, it does not create it** — and its detail pass then overshoots the fix on plains (0.352
+against a real 0.0434, 8× too much) while landing close to right on alpine (0.0403 against 0.0332).
+Two gaps, one underlying cause: **the bake produces almost nothing but smooth interpolation between
+30 m carrier samples, and the client compensates with a class-blind isotropic-in-effect detail
+field.**
+
+**One more measured pipeline defect, upstream of drainage entirely: the coarse carrier discards
+information the model produced.** A B-spline *approximates* its control points; feeding it raw
+samples low-passes the source. The coarse tier does exactly that (raw samples in, no prefilter)
+while the fine tier ships prefiltered control points precisely to avoid it — on an alpine tile the
+coarse carrier was measured to discard **903 mm** of model output at a single 30 m node. This is a
+restatement, with a real-tile number attached, of the "B-spline prefilter is required" finding
+already recorded under Layer 1 above.
+
+**The discrimination table — which metrics can be trusted, and for what.** Against a
+spectrum-matched surrogate (a Gaussian random field carrying a real scene's own variogram to within
+15% at every lag, same variance, same roughness at every scale, no drainage network at all — the
+specific "textbook-correct spectrum, structurally meaningless" failure mode this document opened
+with), only four metric families see through the fake:
+
+| metric | real range | surrogate range | verdict |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `fill_volume_per_area_m` (depression-fill depth) | 0.07–0.97 m | 19.9–64.8 m | **sees through**, 15×, no overlap |
+| `curvature_asymmetry` (\|Laplacian quantile skew\|) | 0.010–0.111 | < 0.003 | **sees through**, 10–100× |
+| `hack_h` (Hack's law exponent) | 0.475–0.557 | 0.161–0.287 | **sees through**, ~2×, no overlap |
+| `tail_asymmetry` (curvature p01/p99) | 0.87–1.32 | 0.99–1.00 | **sees through**, small but clean |
+| `theta` (slope–area concavity) | 0.18–0.40 | 0.13–0.15 | marginal — consistent ~1.8× gap, but only 2.6× the within-scene spread |
+
+**Everything else tested is blind to realism**, including several metrics that read as though they
+should not be: slope statistics (mean/p99/fraction-above-repose all agree with the surrogate to
+2–7%); geomorphon histograms (ridge+peak 0.179 vs. 0.166 on a fluvial scene); the variogram itself,
+by construction; hypsometry (its spread *across five real terrain classes* is smaller than its
+spread *between quadrants of one scene*); drainage density (the real range contains the synthetic
+range at every channel-initiation threshold tried); and raw pit density (a Gaussian field's minima
+are few and enormous, a real landscape's are many and shallow — only the fill *volume* separates
+them, not the count). **Geomorphons flip the other way on the second question**: asked to separate
+five real terrain classes from each other rather than from a fake, `frac_flat` at a 3° threshold
+runs 0.577 High Plains / 0.422 Rub' al Khali / 0.00025 Cumberland Plateau — excellent at class
+identity, blind to realism. **Realism and class-identity are different questions and need different
+metrics**; §6.2 of `terrain-validation-2026-07.md` shows all four realism gates passing on a 30 m
+column that is, by every geomorphon and flat-fraction measure, visibly not the plain it claims to
+be — the realism gates cannot detect a landscape that is internally consistent and of the wrong
+kind, which is exactly the "does it look like Earth" question quoted at face value would miss.
+
+**Read this table as extending, not replacing, "The spectrum is NOT a sufficient acceptance
+criterion" above** — it is the same finding (a textbook-correct summary statistic can describe a
+structurally meaningless surface) now demonstrated over a much wider metric set and pinned to a
+reproducible harness (`terrain-service/tools/geomorph_validate.py --sweep all`) rather than to one
+prototype run.
 
